note "The six design variables. Two are categorical (WheelClass, TrackerGrade), each an\nenumeration whose literals select a concrete alternative of an abstract part with its\nattribute table. Four are continuous (panel span, battery capacity, gains Kp and Kd),\neach with a range sampled log-uniformly; the gains have two boxes, baseline and\naggressive. Ranges are the model's; the sampler in Python reads them from here. " as c1
c1 .. n1
note "Public cubesat wheel classes (illustrative): small ~ 0.3 mN.m, medium ~ 2 mN.m,\nlarge ~ 10 mN.m peak torque; momentum storage 10, 30, 100 mN.m.s (momentum-to-torque\nratios of 33, 15 and 10 s); mass 0.12, 0.25, 0.60 kg. The set spans a factor of 20 in\ntorque so that the wheel class trades agility against mass and power. " as c2
c2 .. n1
note "Star-tracker grades (illustrative): coarse 30 arcsec / 0.15 kg / 0.4 W, medium\n10 arcsec / 0.35 kg / 0.9 W, fine 2 arcsec / 0.90 kg / 1.5 W. A finer sensor points\nbetter, weighs more and draws more. " as c3
c3 .. n1
note "Ranges (illustrative): panel span 0.3 to 1.0 m per wing (two wings; chord 0.34 m\nso total area 0.2 to 0.68 m^2); battery 20 to 200 Wh. " as c4
c4 .. n13
note "Attitude-loop gains, units N.m/rad (Kp) and N.m.s/rad (Kd). " as c5
c5 .. n15
note "Baseline box: Kp 0.03 to 1.0 and Kd 0.3 to 1.5, a stiff attitude loop that asks for\nmore torque than a cubesat wheel gives on a large slew, so the wheel class binds. " as c6
c6 .. n16
note "Aggressive box (the primary dataset): Kp 0.2 to 2.0, Kd 0.3 to 1.5, with Kd/Kp\nmostly at or below 5, where the wheel class separates the slew time. " as c7
c7 .. n17
note "The cubesat is a composition of fixed parts (bus, antenna, IMU) and of the parts the\ndesign variables select (two solar panels of one span, a battery of one capacity, three\nidentical orthogonal wheels, a tracker, the controller gains). The environment is one\nset of mission constants shared by every configuration. " as c8
c8 .. n1
note "LEO of about 95 minutes: orbital rate 1.13e-3 rad/s; eclipse 0.6 h; a battery may be\ncycled to 0.3 depth of discharge at most (cycle life). " as c9
c9 .. n18
note "Solar chain (illustrative): flux 1361 W/m^2, cell efficiency 0.28, packing factor\n0.7, degradation 0.9, mean incidence factor 0.5; the product is about 120 W/m^2 of\npanel, averaged over the sunlit arc. " as c10
c10 .. n18
note "Loads (illustrative): housekeeping and payload 12 W; idle power 0.3 W per wheel;\nwheel electrical loss coefficient c = 5e4 W/(N.m)^2, one value for every class, so\na saturated large wheel (10 mN.m) draws 5 W and a saturated small one 12.5 mW. " as c11
c11 .. n18
note "Disturbance and areal density (illustrative): constant LEO disturbance torque\n(aerodynamic plus solar) 2e-6 N.m; worst-case attitude offset 0.1 degree (1.745e-3 rad) for\nthe gravity-gradient bound; panel areal density 2.5 kg/m^2 per wing including cells and\nsubstrate; battery pack specific energy 140 Wh/kg. " as c12
c12 .. n18
note "12U-class body 0.226 x 0.226 x 0.340 m; bus structure, avionics, harness: 10 kg. " as c13
c13 .. n20
note "Payload antenna: disc of 0.2 m diameter at 0.25 m offset, 0.5 kg (fixed). " as c14
c14 .. n21
note "Inertial measurement unit, 0.1 kg (fixed). " as c15
c15 .. n22
note "PD attitude law tau = -Kp/2 q - Kd omega per axis, clipped at the usable torque.\nDefaults are the lower corner of the baseline gain box. " as c16
c16 .. n23
note "One wing: span (a design variable) by a fixed chord of 0.34 m. " as c17
c17 .. n24
note "Pack mass follows from the capacity (a design variable) and the specific energy. " as c18
c18 .. n25
note "Mass: total = bus + battery + both panels + antenna + 3 wheels + tracker + IMU. The\nstructure mass is what the inertia model calls the body: the total without the panels\nand the antenna, which the inertia model places by themselves. Power: generation is\nproportional to panel area; the power left after the housekeeping and tracker loads is\nwhat the wheels can use, and it caps the usable torque. Energy: the slew loss energy is\nc times the integral of |tau|^2 dt (measured, section 6); the eclipse demand adds the\nbase load over the eclipse; depth of discharge is demand over capacity. " as c19
c19 .. n1
note "A scenario is a named part of numbers: the slew the experiment simulates and the\nbands that read the response. The ODE (adcs_sim.simulate_adcs) is the measurement of\nrecord; the closed-form calcs below approximate its slew time, to say something about\nagility over the whole space (model checking) without simulating. " as c20
c20 .. n1
note "Nominal slew (illustrative): 30 degrees about the body X axis from rest, simulated\nover a 600 s horizon at a 0.5 s step. The slew time is the first time the attitude\nerror falls below a 1 degree band (a 0.5 degree band is recorded for sensitivity);\nthe settling time is the last time the error exceeds 1 % of its start. " as c21
c21 .. n56
note "Closed-form slew time to the band, for the single slew axis (small-angle PD law, torque\ncap T = the usable wheel torque, inertia J, stiffness Kp/4 on the angle, damping Kd):\ntorque-limited regime: bang-bang, accelerate at T/J then brake, which enters a band b\nof an angle a at sqrt(J/T) (2 sqrt(a) - sqrt(2 b));\nlinear regime: the dominant pole of J th'' + Kd th' + (Kp/4) th = 0 (rate Kd/(2J) when\nunderdamped, wn (z - sqrt(z^2 - 1)) when overdamped, wn = sqrt(Kp/(4J)), z =\nKd/sqrt(J Kp)), entering the band at ln(a/b) / rate.\nThe estimate is the larger of the two; the regime is torque-limited when the first\nbinds. Against the ODE it is a model-checking approximation, not a replacement. " as c22
c22 .. n1
note "The four feasibility flags (settled, momentum_ok, power_ok, dod_ok) as named\nrequirements, `satisfy` statements tying them to the baseline, `verification def`s\nnaming what each verifies, and claims over every in-range configuration. " as c23
c23 .. n1
note "Feasibility is stated here and verified, not computed in Python. Four requirements, one\nper flag the experiment reports: settled, momentum_ok, power_ok, dod_ok. A requirement\ntakes a Cubesat as its subject and, where it needs a measured quantity of the slew, an\n`in` parameter that the runner binds by name (a Quantity, or a Real for the integral of\n|tau|^2). The defaults of those parameters are the recorded measurement of the baseline\n(below), so the `satisfy` statements are checkable without a runner. A fifth requirement\n(the slew band was entered) reads the censoring of the slew time; it is not a flag. " as c24
c24 .. n1
